   "failure_scenario": "When many PRs that the factory has no record of already use the same 'change/<title>' branch names, each new run makes one GitHub lookup per taken name while holding the repository lock. This slows down other runs on that repository but always finishes.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: needs GitHub PR lookups; code read instead",
   "evidence": "repos.ts:362-374: `for (let suffix = 2; recorded.has(branch) || taken.has(...) || (repo.kind === 'github' && (await gh lookup)) ...; suffix++)` has no upper bound. The claim is overstated, though. Branches recorded in the store short-circuit before any lookup, so past factory runs cost nothing even after a cache rebuild. A failed lookup throws (withGitHubRetry or remoteSh) rather than looping. The loop ends after at most as many probes as there are existing PRs on those names, so the cost is only latency under the lock."
